This file contains rules for AI code editor to comply with, not related to business logic of an app in development.

# Project Architecture and Structure

## Objective
Build a scalable AI-agent system that leverages Crew AI for backend agent orchestration and Agno AI framework for enhanced AI capabilities, integrated with a modern Next.js frontend. The system aims to provide a user-friendly AI agentic app / system that will allow user to ask agent to work on user's behalf and user can see the agents progress and can interrupt in-between (if required) like human in loop pattern. For communication between frontend and agentic backend, the project use copilotkit capabilities.

## Tech Stack

### Frontend (under `ui` folder)
- Next.js (React framework)
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- Copilotkit
- ReactJS
- Jest & React Testing Library
- Cypress for E2E testing

### Backend (under `api` folder)
- Python 3.11+
- FastAPI
- Agno AI Framework
- SQLAlchemy ORM
- PostgreSQL / Supabase
- Redis for caching
- Pytest
- Docker
- Kubernetes for orchestration

## Directory Organization
- /frontend: Frontend codebase (Next.js, Tailwind, Agno AI)
  - /components: Reusable UI components
  - /lib: Shared utilities and hooks
  - /app: Page components and routing
  - /public: Static assets

- /backend: Backend codebase (Python)
  - /routes: API endpoints
  - /models: Data models
  - /services: Business logic
  - /utils: Helper functions

## Naming Conventions

### Frontend (TypeScript/JavaScript)
- Components: PascalCase (e.g., UserProfile.tsx)
- Files: kebab-case (e.g., auth-utils.ts)
- Functions: camelCase (e.g., handleSubmit)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- CSS Classes: lowercase with hyphens

### Backend (Python)
- Files: snake_case (e.g., user_service.py)
- Classes: PascalCase
- Functions/Variables: snake_case
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE

## Code Quality Standards

### Frontend
- Use TypeScript for type safety
- Implement proper component composition
- Follow React hooks best practices
- Maintain consistent Tailwind class ordering
- Use Agno AI framework conventions

### Backend
- Follow PEP 8 style guide
- Use type hints
- Implement proper error handling
- Write comprehensive docstrings
- Use dependency injection

## Documentation Requirements
- Component documentation with props interface
- API endpoint documentation with request/response schemas
- Setup and deployment instructions in README
- Code comments for complex logic
- Architecture decisions in .talks folder

## Testing Standards

### Frontend
- Unit tests for components
- Integration tests for pages
- E2E tests for critical flows
- Test coverage minimum: 80%

### Backend
- Unit tests for services
- Integration tests for APIs
- Mock external dependencies
- Test coverage minimum: 85%

## Performance Guidelines
- Implement code splitting
- Optimize image loading
- Use proper caching strategies
- Monitor and optimize API response times
- Regular performance audits

## Security Practices
- Input validation
- XSS prevention
- CSRF protection
- Secure authentication
- Regular security audits

## Version Control
- Semantic versioning
- Descriptive commit messages
- Feature branch workflow
- Pull request reviews
- CI/CD pipeline checks

## Special Notes
- For any frontend / ui related changes or commands always consider that the change needs to be done or command needs to run in `ui` folder.
- For any backend related changes or commands always consider that the change needs to be done or command needs to run in `api` folder
- For python requirements file, lets have two file `requirements.in` and `requirements.txt`. `.in` file should have packages that are required (either with version or otherwise), its file in flux. `.txt` file is like froozen file with working packages and exact version numbers. Ensure to have dev dependecies mantained separately so that in prod they dont get installed.
- Documentation for some of the tech stack is available under `.docs` folder. Refer it as needed.
- For running any `crew`, `pip`, `uv` or similar backend related terminal commands, always first activate the virtual env or run `activate` command to activate it and then run the command as part of single command separated by &&, as the AI code editor opens a separate terminal window for each command.
- In place of `pip`, try to use `uv`, if possible.